<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">English Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1642">1642</a>–<a href="/years/1651">1651</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The English Civil War was a series of wars from 1642 to 1651 between the supporters of King Charles I and of Parliament, part of the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland. Parliament's victory led to the trial and execution of Charles I in 1649, the abolition of the monarchy and the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuart-restoration">Restoration of 1660</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/english-civil-war.jpg" alt="English Civil War" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Naseby,_hand-coloured_copper_engraving.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The wars broke out in 1642, less than forty years after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>, who was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> as James I of England, creating the first personal union of the two kingdoms. Accustomed to a weak parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, James was affronted by the constraints the English Parliament tried to place on him in return for money. His extravagance forced him to seek income outside Parliament. The English war was fought primarily over the proper balance of power between Parliament and Charles I. The Royalists held the countryside, the shires and the less developed north and west, and Parliament the industrial centres, ports and richer regions of the south and east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In early January 1642, a few days after failing to capture five members of the House of Commons, Charles left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> area for the north. On 1 June the Lords and Commons approved the Nineteen Propositions, in which Parliament sought a larger share of power, and the king rejected them before the month was out. The first war ended in June 1646 with Royalist defeat and the king in custody. On 28 December 1647 he made a secret treaty with the Scots, the Engagement, under which they would invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on his behalf, and Royalist uprisings and a Scottish invasion followed in 1648.</p><p>In December 1648 the Army carried out Pride's Purge, arresting 45 members of Parliament and keeping 146 out of the chamber. The remaining Rump Parliament set up a High Court of Justice to try Charles for treason, and Fairfax, a constitutional monarchist, refused to take part and resigned as head of the army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> on 15 August 1649 to quell an alliance of Irish Confederates and English Royalists, whom his forces had already beaten at the Battle of Rathmines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 1650 Charles II was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, in return for agreeing to a Presbyterian church in both kingdoms, and the Anglo-Scottish war ended with a Parliamentarian victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> on 3 September 1651. Ireland and Scotland were incorporated into the Commonwealth, an arrangement that proved unpopular and was dissolved at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuart-restoration">Restoration of 1660</a>. Figures for casualties are unreliable, but a conservative estimate is some 190,000 dead out of a population of about five million. The wars popularised the terms Cavalier and Roundhead and brought a great growth in pamphlets and newsbooks, and the outcome set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Scotland on course towards a parliamentary monarchy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “English Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>English Civil War</b> Jersey held out for the king under Sir George Carteret until Parliamentary forces under Admiral Robert Blake and Colonel James Heane conquered it in 1651.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Thomas Hobbes drew the same lesson from the <b>English Civil War</b>: only a sovereign whom no one could lawfully resist could guarantee the peace on which every other good depended.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Its ideas began to coalesce during the <b>English Civil War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Jean Bodin, writing during the French Wars of Religion, argued in 1576 that sovereignty, the absolute and perpetual power of a commonwealth, must be undivided, and Thomas Hobbes drew the same lesson from the <b>English Civil War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote>A Scottish Covenanter army under Alexander Leslie, Earl of Leven, laid siege to Hereford, one of the remaining Royalist strongholds of the <b>English Civil War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-30">30 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles I's army fought Parliament's forces under the Earl of Essex at Edgehill in Warwickshire, the first pitched battle of the <b>English Civil War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-23">23 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
